hi I'm Annie whoo and I'm a mathematics  teacher I think one of the biggest  challenges at the moment in teaching  mathematics is that in Australia our  syllabus is not really structured to  encourage mastery in fact it's  structured to encourage people to move  on through a series of ideas and be  exposed to them but not necessarily you  really get them at a conceptual level  and to illustrate how this would look  different I mean if you ask someone you  know oh you play the piano and you're  grade eight you know how old are you  well the answer is however old they want  to be there might be six years old or 16  or 60 and we say you can play that level  of piano whenever you are ready for and  when you can develop those skills but  somehow we say you're 15 years old you  must know trigonometry by now that's  crazy to me when everyone has such a  different mind such a different set of  gifts and talents and skills and also  such a different journey that they've  come from different contexts where the  background the different teaching that  they have been able to encounter will  they've given them a deeper or a more  shallow experience of those different  concepts so for me to be able to say to  a student I am going to give you help  you experience the learning that you  need now that makes sense in your  particular instant in time for me the  structures around the syllabus make that  very difficult because I standardized  testing means we've got to move on  forward you're getting into stage six  well I guess I have to finish all Stage  five whether you're ready for that or  not so for me that's the big struggle  and I know that's a really difficult  thing to work with because we have  curriculum requirements at the same time  I think we owe it to our students that  we need to wrestle with that and not  just say sorry this is the best that we  can give you I don't think it is I think  we can push on that gently to try and  expand the envelope of what our students  can experience so it makes sense to them  at their moment  you
 

